<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=qanat">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qanat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A qanat is an underground channel that carries water by gravity from mountain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquifer">aquifers</a> to lowland fields and settlements. The technique originated in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and spread across arid regions. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> qanat irrigation enabled cultivation across the dry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a> and supported a predominantly sedentary peasant population.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qanat.jpg" alt="Qanat" width="300" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tavasoli mohsen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghasabe_Qanats_of_Gonabad2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A qanat, or kariz, was developed in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, originating approximately 3,000 years ago. According to most sources, Iranians created the technology in the early 1st millennium BCE and it spread slowly westward and eastward; other sources suggest a south-eastern Arabian origin, and similar systems appear to have been developed independently in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. One theory holds that the qanat was devised to irrigate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> fields in what is now Iran, doubling the water available for irrigation and urban use, which gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> larger agricultural surpluses and so more urbanisation and social stratification.</p><p>The word comes from Akkadian qanû, meaning reed. It is called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foggara">foggara</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> and falaj in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>. Qanat-style systems reached parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and were built on a larger scale in Muslim-ruled Spain. The Spanish introduced qanats into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1520, and similar works at Nazca, called puquios, are dated by some archaeologists to about 500 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>A qanat is a gently sloping tunnel reached by a series of well-like vertical shafts. It taps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundwater-depletion">groundwater</a>, usually from an upland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquifer">aquifer</a>, and delivers it by gravity to a lower point without pumping. Because it runs underground, little water is lost to evaporation, and the system is fairly resistant to floods, earthquakes and wartime destruction.</p><p>Skilled labourers called muqannīs built qanats by hand, a well-paid profession usually passed from father to son. A crew of three or four began at the outlet and worked towards a test well dug where the mountains meet the alluvial fan, sinking shafts 20 to 35 metres apart. Fired clay hoops lined soft ground, and the shafts had to be cleaned and inspected regularly.</p><p>In Iranian towns, which often have several qanats, the water set the layout. The cleanest water was upstream, and more prosperous people lived at or just above the outlet. Water below the outlet ran in surface canals called jubs, and the streets followed them. Qanats supplied irrigation, drinking water and watermills, and air from them cooled an underground summer room, the shabestan. By 400 BCE Persian engineers could store ice through the summer in yakhchals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Qanats still supply water in hot, arid regions. Among the largest working systems, besides those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, are those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> water system in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>. In Afghanistan more than 9,370 were in use in the twentieth century. Many long-inhabited settlements of West Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> stand where conditions favoured a qanat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qanat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They improved irrigation with <b>qanats</b> and introduced lucrative crops, including oranges, lemons, pistachio and sugarcane, as well as vegetables, dates and mulberry trees where only wheat had been grown for centuries, and mining began.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Iranian agriculture depended on the <b>qanat</b> system of underground channels carrying water from mountain aquifers to lowland fields.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The atabegs ruled a small oasis state around Yazd and Abarquh, living from irrigated farming by <b>qanats</b>, silk weaving and caravan trade across the central desert.</blockquote><a href="/state/atabegs-of-yazd">Atabegs of Yazd</a></li><li><blockquote>Abdallah ibn Tahir (828 to 845) was known for justice, protection of peasants and regulation of the <b>qanat</b> irrigation system.</blockquote><a href="/state/tahirid-dynasty">Tahirid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 1000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Iran</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Nazca, Peru (puquios) (c. 500 CE)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Some sources propose an origin in south-east Arabia, and the dating of the Peruvian puquios is disputed.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/irrigation">Irrigation<span>c. 6000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=qanat">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
